WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.369
Our next guest
is an Emmy-winning writer

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.471
who has worked on
"The Office" and "Hacks,"

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:07.006
created "The Good Place,"
and co-created both

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.642
"Parks and Recreation"
and "Brooklyn Nine Nine."

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:13.112
One day,
he's gonna break through.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:14.714
One day,
he's gonna work on a show

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:17.650
that breaks through
with the American people.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.953
His book, "How to Be Perfect:
The Correct Answer

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.921
to Every Moral Question,"
is available now.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.289
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.358
our very good friend
Michael Schur, everybody.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:27.427
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:33.933
♪♪

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:40.440
♪♪

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.208
Welcome back, my friend.
-Thank you, buddy.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.475
How are you?
-I'm wonderful.

00:00:43.475 --> 00:00:45.411
This is a fantastic book.

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:48.214
Obviously inspired by the
creation of "The Good Place."

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.149
-Right.
-At what point while you were

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:52.852
writing a television show
about what it means to be good

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.121
did you think, "I also want
to write a book about it"?

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.158
-Sort of toward the end.
We were in the last season.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:01.160
And I really liked
reading all this stuff,

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.395
and I liked talking about it
with smart, funny people.

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:04.998
And at the end I was like,
you know what?

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:06.699
What I want to do is, like,

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:09.269
conduct an exit interview
for myself kind of

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.204
to see what have I learned
and how can I express it.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:16.575
So I pitched the book
almost exactly two years ago

00:01:16.575 --> 00:01:19.345
and sold it and then thought,
like, "Man, I don't know

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:21.947
if I have the time to do this."

00:01:21.947 --> 00:01:24.984
And then you might remember,
there was a pandemic...

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:27.020
-Yeah.
-...that swept across the globe.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.823
And so it became
my pandemic project.

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:33.092
I was just in my office alone
and at home, pounding away.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:34.526
-You obviously have had

00:01:34.526 --> 00:01:36.729
incredible success writing
episodic television.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.765
I don't think there's
ever a code to crack,

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.134
but the more you do it,
the better you must get at it.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.902
Did you find writing a book

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.536
wildly different
than working on television?

00:01:44.536 --> 00:01:45.538
-Oh, it's so much harder.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:46.872
-It's so much harder
to write a book?

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:48.708
-It's so much harder.
Yeah, because when you write

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:51.211
a TV show, you are in a room
with like 12 people

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:53.212
who are smart and funny,
and you pitch a joke,

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:55.080
and then they pitch jokes
that are better.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.715
And you go, "Well,
let's go with your joke."

00:01:56.715 --> 00:01:59.018
And when you're writing a book,
you write a joke,

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.253
and then you're like,
"Ah. This is all I got."

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:02.655
Like, I don't know.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.958
There were a couple times
I literally e-mailed

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:08.027
the writing staff
of "The Good Place"

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.596
and was like, "I wrote
this joke. It sucks.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:10.763
Can you make a better one?"

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.465
And they were like,
"Sure, here."

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:15.201
I was like, "Oh, God. I would
kill to have that room back."

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:18.437
-Was ethics something that
you thought about much before?

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:19.973
You must have thought about it

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.407
when you created
"The Good Place,"

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.544
but has it always been something
that you were interested in?

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:27.613
-Yeah, I was a rules dork
my whole life.

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:29.649
Like, when I was
in kindergarten,

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:32.418
if the teacher said,
"Okay, everybody, line up,"

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.687
I would line up immediately
and then I would look around

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.722
at the other kids like,
"What are you doing?

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:37.923
What are you doing?

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:39.492
She just said line up.
Did you not hear her?"

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.295
So I was always the kid
who followed every rule,

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:44.297
so, clearly,
it was baked in somewhere.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.266
And then over my life, a bunch
of things have happened to me

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:49.702
in small and big ways
that have made me get interested

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.003
in the subject.

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:53.506
And then the show was obviously,
like, the culmination

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.474
of that, was really me saying,

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:57.143
"Okay, I'm gonna focus on this
and explicitly talk about it."

00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:00.079
-Have you ever done something
and in the moment wrestled with,

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.181
or observed from the outside
as you were doing it,

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:05.585
"Oh, why -- this is --
Why am I doing this?"

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:08.287
-All the time. Do you not?
-Yeah, sure.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:10.155
-Yeah, I feel like that --

00:03:10.155 --> 00:03:13.326
To me, being alive on Earth
means doing things

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.962
and then immediately saying,
"Why did I do that?

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.696
What is -- Why did I do that?"

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:22.735
So the study of ethics is really
just getting some vocabulary

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:25.271
and learning some theories
about what the hell we're doing

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:26.906
and why we're doing it
and trying to be better.

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.642
-What about
doing the right thing

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.778
but because you want credit
for doing the right thing?

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:34.280
You have caught yourself
doing that?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.381
-I have.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.550
One of the things
that inspired the show

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.253
and then the book was that
I used to go to Starbucks

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.421
near my house
and I would get coffee.

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:44.290
It was like $1.73,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.159
and I would throw the money
in the tip jar,

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.659
except I realized, one day,

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.596
I didn't just throw the money
in the tip jar.

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:52.931
I waited until the barista
turned around and was facing me

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:55.067
so that I was like,
"Yes, that's right."

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:58.270
You know, like, "Here.
I give you 27 cents."

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.105
And when I caught myself
doing it

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:02.641
and realized I had been
doing it for a long time,

00:04:02.641 --> 00:04:04.710
it was like,
"Why do I want this credit?"

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.147
That is such a weird thing to
want, 27 cents worth of credit.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.950
And that led me into a long,
like, rabbit hole

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.918
that I write about it the book

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:14.687
if you're interested in reading
what I discovered.

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.689
-It must have been weird
to write this book

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:19.192
during a pandemic where there
were a lot of these moral,

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.693
ethical questions, as far as how

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.496
our behavior
affects other people.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:25.097
Everyone during this pandemic

00:04:25.097 --> 00:04:27.700
was asked to make sacrifices
for the greater good.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.335
And we have seen, obviously,

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.737
these divergent paths
that people have chosen to take.

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:34.373
Was it hard not to write
specifically about the pandemic?

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:36.408
-Yeah, it was awfully hard
not to make the whole book

00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:38.544
about the pandemic, because
every time I'd be discussing

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:39.878
some kind of theory,
it would be like,

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:42.214
what's the right thing
to use to explain?

00:04:42.214 --> 00:04:43.449
Oh, it's the pandemic.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.118
It's the thing that
everyone is going through.

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:46.652
And there are a couple points
in the book

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:48.620
where I talk about that,
but things like not wanting to

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.223
wear masks and not wanting
to get vaccinated --

00:04:51.223 --> 00:04:53.392
there's a famous problem
in philosophy

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.361
called the free-rider problem
which is basically, like,

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.664
everybody pays for a ride
on a trolley,

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:59.431
and you run up
and jump onto the outside

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:01.167
and grab hold
and get a free ride.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.702
And it's like,
yeah, that's not cool.

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:04.637
Technically, everybody
got where they were going,

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:06.839
but everybody else did the thing
they were supposed to do

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:08.274
and you didn't.

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:10.843
And once you learn about
these problems in philosophy,

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:12.278
you see them everywhere.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:15.581
And every time somebody started
screaming in a Jamba Juice

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.850
about how their freedom
was being trampled, I was like,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.252
"Alright, buddy, here's this
theory, here's that theory.

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:21.888
Here's why
what you're doing is..."

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:24.356
-And most of those people
who scream in Jamba Juices

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:25.724
love hearing theories.

00:05:25.724 --> 00:05:27.126
-Oh, that's
their favorite thing.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.861
They love philosophy,
those folks.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.763
-They love -- Yeah.
-Yeah, you can go in and you can

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.232
engage them in a philosophy
discussion pretty quickly.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:34.867
Yeah.
-You did some -- First of all,

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:37.069
you know,
I've known you a long time.

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.972
I've never -- Again,
this is not unique to you.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:42.775
A lot of people grew their
hair out during the pandemic.

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.344
-Yeah.
-And let me just say, I mean,

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:47.980
some people grew it out
and looked bad.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.415
I mean, you looked fantastic.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.483
-Thank you. Thank you. Yes.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:54.052
-And it's just like --
A lot of people have, like,

00:05:54.052 --> 00:05:56.088
a long hair style
and it only looks good one way.

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:57.256
-No, not in my case.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.725
-Yeah, this is a real --
This is a twofer.

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.495
-Yeah. Now, when you said
grow your hair out,

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:04.830
it's more accurate to say "just
didn't get your hair cut."

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.931
-That's true.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:07.166
-Like, I just didn't
get my hair cut.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:08.634
-Yeah. Very well done.
-Thank you.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.070
-You also -- You know,
an accomplished actor.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.138
-Thank you.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:14.106
-Now I'm asking
a genuine question.

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:17.643
Obviously, the enduring
popularity of your shows,

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:20.546
not just "The Good Place" which,
again, I hear all the time --

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.348
-You haven't seen it,
but you hear all the time.

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:24.083
-I hear all the time
that I got to watch it.

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:26.185
Now, is it on -- What's it on?
Where can I see it?

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:28.320
-It was on NBC.
-It was on this --

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.022
-It was.
It was on this network.

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:31.190
-I'm sorry.
-It's okay, buddy.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.860
-I don't get NBC.
-Look -- [ Laughs ]

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.228
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:38.597
What is your audio/video setup
at home?

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:40.199
-What do I have?
-Yeah.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:44.737
-I have Apple TV Minus.
-I see. I see.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.105
-It's all shows that have been
cancelled by Apple.

00:06:47.105 --> 00:06:48.674
-[ Laughs ]
You're making a good living.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.810
I would upgrade to
at least Apple TV Neutral.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.211
-No. No, thanks.

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.081
I just feel like TV's
gotten too liberal.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:57.082
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:58.851
So you are
also an accomplished actor.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:06:59.918
-Yes.

00:06:59.918 --> 00:07:01.120
-You played Mose
on "The Office."

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.521
-Correct.
-[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:05.024
-Now -- There we go.

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:09.361
Now, I say this because when
John Krasinski's on the show,

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:10.829
when Rainn Wilson
is on the show,

00:07:10.829 --> 00:07:13.899
when BJ Novak is on the show,
when they get introduced,

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:16.869
the audience goes crazy,
and it's because the audience

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:19.005
oftentimes is watching
"The Office" now.

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:21.240
-Sure.
-And to a person, they always

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.043
say, "This is applause I never
got when 'The Office' aired."

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.778
-Right.
-And people are -- so are --

00:07:26.778 --> 00:07:28.881
Do people recognize you as Mose
now more than ever?

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.517
-Ten times more than they did
at the time, yeah.

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:34.052
And because Mose,
if you remember,

00:07:34.052 --> 00:07:36.622
had an unpleasant
neckbeard situation.

00:07:36.622 --> 00:07:38.257
There it is.
-We've got a picture.

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:40.393
-Yeah, so...
-[ Laughter ]

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:44.663
The neckbeard serves
as a reverse disguise,

00:07:44.663 --> 00:07:46.298
where when a lot of people
see me

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:49.802
they don't think of Mose because
they don't see that horrible...

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:50.969
-Yeah.

00:07:50.969 --> 00:07:53.105
When you take a mask off
and hold it here,

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:55.340
do they go, "Mose"?

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.844
-I should get a mask
that has that as a print

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:01.580
so that when I pull it down,
it's like, "Hello."

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.682
-This looks like a guy in
a Jamba Juice who pulled down

00:08:03.682 --> 00:08:06.786
his mask and was like, "I didn't
know this was Germany!"

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:08.487
[ Laughter ]

00:08:08.487 --> 00:08:12.090
-Yeah, so, but now there -- I
do, I would say, get recognized

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.693
ten times more than I did
when the show was on the air.

00:08:14.693 --> 00:08:17.663
-I bring this up because
you then did a fundraiser.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:20.099
-Yes.
-And basically the conceit

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:22.367
of the fundraiser was the more
money you raised --

00:08:22.367 --> 00:08:24.270
if people --
the more they donated,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.473
the more likely you would be
to do something embarrassing.

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:28.874
-It started
with shaving my head.

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:30.342
It was like if we raised
50 grand --

00:08:30.342 --> 00:08:32.211
This was for
the L.A. Regional Food Bank.

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.513
If we raised 50 grand,
I will shave my head.

00:08:34.513 --> 00:08:37.483
I made that comment on Twitter
without talking to my wife

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:39.485
or thinking for one second
about it.

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:41.153
And then that happened.

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:42.488
And then what I realized

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:44.556
was the humiliation
was what people were after.

00:08:44.556 --> 00:08:47.059
So it it became if we raise this
much, I'll do this or whatever.

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:49.362
I eventually changed
the shaving of the head

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.263
to regrowing the Mose beard,

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:53.699
wearing a Derek Jeter jersey,
because I'm a Red Sox fan,

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:56.668
and then people who donated
a certain amount --

00:08:56.668 --> 00:08:58.938
including, by the way, this guy.
-Yeah.

00:08:58.938 --> 00:09:03.142
-Could have me say
embarrassing things on camera

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:05.077
while I ate a hot apple pie.

00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:07.345
Which I hate. I hate hot fruit.

00:09:07.345 --> 00:09:09.582
So I ate a hot apple pie.

00:09:09.582 --> 00:09:13.385
-And America.
I always forget that.

00:09:13.385 --> 00:09:15.554
-But I love baseball. I love --
That's one out of three.

00:09:15.554 --> 00:09:16.822
-You love baseball enough

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:18.624
to make up for how much
you hate apple pie.

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:21.727
-So I wore a Derek Jeter jersey,
grew the Mose beard,

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:24.196
ate hot apple pie, and said
embarrassing things on a video.

00:09:24.196 --> 00:09:25.798
-And here's a photo of you.

00:09:25.798 --> 00:09:27.833
Look. Just Mose doing great.

00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:29.968
I like to think that,
in the future, Mose has done

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:32.605
so well that he has a nice TV.

00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:36.609
-I like that just Mose's beard
is so gray.

00:09:36.609 --> 00:09:38.677
That's the sense. "Oh, I guess
he made it to old age."

00:09:38.677 --> 00:09:40.779
-We are both Red Sox fans.
We connected on that.

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:42.615
We have a difference --

00:09:42.615 --> 00:09:45.251
You're a huge Patriots fan.
I'm a Pittsburgh Steelers fan.

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:46.885
Will you just share
with everyone what --

00:09:46.885 --> 00:09:49.455
I made a donation to a food bank
and then I got you to say...

00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:52.557
-I don't even remember --
I said a lot of things about --

00:09:52.557 --> 00:09:53.993
-It was about Spygate.

00:09:53.993 --> 00:09:56.528
-Spygate was real.
-Spygate was real.

00:09:56.528 --> 00:09:58.830
And it meant the world to me
that you finally admitted that.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.799
-Yeah. Yeah.
Well, you're welcome.

00:10:00.799 --> 00:10:03.068
I'm happy that money
went to a food bank.

00:10:03.068 --> 00:10:04.703
I don't know
what you want me to say.

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:06.639
-Hey, buddy, thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:06.639 --> 00:10:08.139
It's always nice
to talk with you.

00:10:08.139 --> 00:10:09.775
Michael Schur, everybody.

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:12.244
"How to Be Perfect"
is available now.

